("premiere",None,"The Magic Flute premiered on 30 September 1791 in Vienna, just two months before Mozart\u2019s death.","The work premiered on 30 September 1791 at Schikaneder's theatre, the Freihaus-Theater auf der Wieden in Vienna, just two months before Mozart's death."),
("premiere",None,"At the premiere Mozart conducted, the librettist Schikaneder played Papageno himself, and the Queen of the Night was sung by Mozart\u2019s sister-in-law, Josepha Hofer.","Mozart conducted the orchestra and Schikaneder himself played Papageno, while the role of the Queen of the Night was sung by Mozart's sister-in-law Josepha Hofer.","Premiere_and_reception"),
("composer",None,"Mozart to his wife on 7 October 1791: \u201cI have this moment returned from the opera, which was as full as ever \u2026 what always gives me the most pleasure is the silent approval!\u201d",["I have this moment returned from the opera, which was as full as ever","But what always gives me the most pleasure is the silent approval!"],"Premiere_and_reception","Mozart, letter to Constanze (via Wikipedia)"),
("composer",None,"Mozart went to hear his new opera almost every night, taking along friends and relatives.","He went to hear his opera almost every night","Premiere_and_reception"),
("history",None,"The opera reached its 100th performance in November 1792, but Mozart didn\u2019t live to see it: he died on 5 December 1791.","The opera celebrated its 100th performance in November 1792, though Mozart did not have the pleasure of witnessing this milestone, as he had died on 5 December 1791.","Premiere_and_reception"),
("music","Overture|Ouvert","The overture was written after the rest of the opera. Its three solemn brass chords nod to Freemasonry, where the number three is highly significant.",["The overture, composed after the other parts of the opera were complete, begins with a solemn three-chord sequence from the brass","The number three is highly significant in Freemasonry"],"Overture"),
("history",None,"Both Mozart and Schikaneder were Freemasons, and the opera is noted for its Masonic elements.",["The Magic Flute is noted for its prominent Masonic elements","Schikaneder and Mozart were Freemasons"],"Themes"),
("music","Der Vogelf\u00e4nger|Vogelfanger|Papageno","Papageno\u2019s whistle is based on a motif borrowed from a concerto by Antonio Salieri.","Papageno's whistle is based on a motif borrowed from Salieri's Concerto for Clavicembalo in B♭ major.","Composition"),
("music","Pa-?pa-?pa|Papagena","The Papageno\u2013Papagena duet, with its bird-like syllables, resembles a comic cavatina in Salieri\u2019s Prima la musica e poi le parole.","the Papageno–Papagena duet is similar to the Cucuzze cavatina in Salieri's Prima la musica e poi le parole","Composition"),
("music","Das klinget|Glockenspiel|bells","Mozart called for a \u201cstromento d\u2019acciaio\u201d (instrument of steel) to play Papageno\u2019s magic bells.","Mozart also called for a stromento d'acciaio (instrument of steel) to perform Papageno's magic bells.","Orchestration"),
("music","Der, welcher wandert|Armed Men|armou?red men|Geharnischte","The Armed Men\u2019s chorale is set to a Baroque chorale prelude, inspired by Martin Luther\u2019s hymn \u201cAch Gott, vom Himmel sieh darein\u201d.","sung to a Baroque chorale prelude, inspired by Martin Luther's hymn \"Ach Gott, vom Himmel sieh darein\"","Scene_7:_At_the_Mountains_of_Ordeal"),
("critic",None,"Pauline Kael called it \u201ca peerlessly silly masterpiece: sublimely lucid music arising out of a parodistic fairy tale.\u201d","It's a peerlessly silly masterpiece: sublimely lucid music arising out of a parodistic fairy tale","Premiere_and_reception","Pauline Kael (via Wikipedia)"),
("history","Der H\u00f6lle Rache|Der Holle Rache|O zittre nicht","Some see the Queen of the Night as a portrait of Empress Maria Theresa, who banned Freemasonry from Austria.","by others to represent Roman Catholic Empress Maria Theresa, who banned Freemasonry from Austria","Themes"),
